WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.909
SSRF abuses the trust placed in server-side network access.

2
00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:13.780
A feature that fetches URLs, imports images, checks webhooks, or connects to integrations may reach systems that the attacker cannot access directly.

3
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:26.964
Internal network abuse is common because application servers often have access to private services, management endpoints, databases, or service discovery systems that are not exposed to the internet.

4
00:00:26.964 --> 00:00:30.144
Cloud metadata attacks are a major SSRF concern.

5
00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:38.889
If a vulnerable workload can reach metadata services, attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or instance information.

6
00:00:38.889 --> 00:00:44.984
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-supplied destinations as safe.

7
00:00:44.984 --> 00:00:50.019
URL validation, network controls, and egress policies need to work together.

8
00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:55.650
Segment networks so application workloads cannot reach unnecessary internal services.

9
00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.751
Validate and restrict outbound destinations using allow lists where possible.

10
00:01:00.751 --> 00:01:05.323
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual server-initiated requests.

11
00:01:05.323 --> 00:01:14.333
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SC-7 - Boundary Protection; AC-4 - Information Flow Enforcement; SI-4 - System Monitoring.

